  <h1>Direct mail for architects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The average American household got 393 pieces of advertising mail in a year. That is
    about seven and a half a week, and it is down 40 percent in a decade. Now compare that
    with your own inbox this morning.
  </p>

  <p>
    Direct mail gets declared dead every few years by people who never checked the
    arithmetic. Then they go home, sort a small stack of physical mail over the
    recycling bin, and keep the one piece that was worth keeping. The channel did not
    die. It got quiet, which for a marketer is the opposite of dead. Quiet is where
    attention is cheap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those numbers come from the Postal Service's own <a href="https://prc.arkcase.com/api/prc-dockets/filing/downloadFile?fileId=252658&inline=true" rel="nofollow">Household Mail Survey</a> for the year to September 2025 (about 5,000 households keeping a one-week mail diary, published March 2026). Households earning $150,000 or more got the most, 527 pieces a year, around ten a week. So the people who hire architects get a little more mail than average. Still a short stack.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Direct mail works for architects as a follow-up layer, not a discovery engine. In the USPS 2025 mail survey, 25 percent of ad mail from a business the household already knew was rated very likely to get a response, against 3 percent from strangers. So aim it at past inquiries, past clients and referral partners: a well-printed piece of finished work for a warm residential list, a personal letter to the few dozen commercial names that matter. One catch first: across 2,196 leads at 17 firms we run ads for, only 3 gave a street address. Collect addresses before you plan the mailing.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    This page is about where a physical piece fits in the wider system in <a href="/architecture-marketing">architecture marketing</a>, and, more usefully, where it does not. Because direct mail is a genuinely good tool held wrong by almost everyone who reaches for it.
  </p>

  <h2>The one stat that decides everything</h2>
  <p>
    Every year the survey asks households to rate each ad piece they got on a 1 to 10 scale for how likely they are to respond. Then it splits the answers by whether the sender already had a relationship with them.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>USPS Household Mail Survey, FY2025</th><th>From a business they know</th><th>From a stranger</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Pieces read</td><td>64%</td><td>36%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rated very likely to respond (8 to 10)</td><td>25%</td><td>3%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Very likely to respond, with a coupon</td><td>37%</td><td>5%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Very likely to respond, no coupon</td><td>18%</td><td>2%</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Read that twice, because it quietly rewrites what direct mail is for. A piece from a name the household knows is about eight times as likely to get that rating. It will not go find you buyers who have never heard your name. It is one of the best ways ever invented to land, physically, in the hands of someone who already has.
  </p>
  <p>
    One honest caveat: this is stated intent, measured on a ten-point scale, and a promise to respond is cheaper than a phone call. The gap is so wide that the direction survives any discount you want to apply to it. (The coupon rows are for retailers. Please do not send a coupon for a custom home.)
  </p>

  <h2>Which makes it a follow-up channel, not a discovery one</h2>
  <p>
    Most firms get this exactly backwards. They rent a list of ten thousand addresses
    in a wealthy area, blast a postcard, get a handful of calls, and decide direct mail
    does not work. What did not work was mailing strangers and calling it a strategy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The version that works points the same money at a much smaller, much warmer list: the people who inquired last year and went quiet, past clients with another project in them, and the <a href="/referral-systems-for-architects">referral partners</a> who send you work. A real card to a hundred people who know your name beats a cheap postcard to ten thousand who do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    If that sounds a lot like the job of <a href="/email-marketing-for-architects">email marketing</a>, it is the same job in a different material. Email is free, instant, and easy to ignore. Mail costs real money, takes a few days, and is hard to throw out without at least looking at it. Run both at the warm list and you cover the person who deletes and the person who keeps.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>How they differ</th><th>Email</th><th>Direct mail</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost per touch</td><td>Practically zero</td><td>65 cents of postage for a postcard, plus printing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ad pieces in the box</td><td>More than you want to count</td><td>About 7 to 10 a week</td></tr>
    <tr><td>What you need to send it</td><td>An email address (2,194 of our 2,196 leads)</td><td>A street address (3 of 2,196)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ignoring it takes</td><td>One keystroke</td><td>A walk to the recycling bin</td></tr>
